WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.550
Hello.

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:04.520
Today, we'll talk about genome
rearrangements, and we'll be

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.900
focusing on the question, how to transform
man into mice.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.250
Ten years ago when my daughter was just
ten

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.780
years old, she asked what I am doing at
work.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.292
And I told her, I'm transforming men into
mice.

00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:22.500
She wasn't satisfied, and she asked for a
clarification,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.589
and therefore I had to tell her a story.

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:26.130
Men and mice

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:31.660
may look different, but
genetically they are very similar.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:37.450
For nearly every gene in man, there is a
similar gene in mouse.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:43.180
And, mouse of course, outperform us with
respect to smell genes,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:48.300
but we outperform mice a little bit with
respect to brain related genes.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.830
But overall, you can say that men and mice

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:55.730
have roughly the same set of genes.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:01.020
But these genes are arranged differently
in human and mouse genomes.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:06.570
And that's why explaining to my daughter
how man and mice differ genetically,

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:12.090
I was telling her take 23 human
chromosome, cut them

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:17.320
into 280 pieces, shuffle these pieces and
glue them together

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.878
in a new order in 20 mouse chromosomes.
You will get a mouse genome.

00:01:22.878 --> 00:01:27.810
She seemed satisfied, but she asked me if
you can transform

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.980
men into mice, can you transform mice into
man as well.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.045
And I responded, of course it's very easy.

00:01:34.045 --> 00:01:38.040
You just need to reverse this operation of
cutting and gluing,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.370
and you will get, starting from mice, you
will get man.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:47.608
So today we'll focus on a slightly more
simple problem of

00:01:47.608 --> 00:01:54.240
transforming mouse X chromosome into human
X chromosome.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.400
X chromosomes in mammals are special
because genes do

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.420
not jump from the sex chromosome to other
chromosome.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:08.290
And therefore you can think about X
chromosomes in mammals as

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:13.110
separate sub-genomes.
Making it a little bit easier to analyze.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:18.760
And it turns out that human and mouse X
chromosome, despite

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:24.370
the fact that they are very long strings,
150 million nucleotides long, they can

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:30.350
be thought of as just sequences of 11
large segments.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.310
Each of these segments may contain hundreds
of genes

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:38.990
but within each segment
the genes are very similar.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:43.220
However, these segments, called synteny
blocks,

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.350
are arranged differently in mouse and
human.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:52.870
And a number of questions arise.
First, how can we transform a

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:58.368
long strand consisting of 150 million
nucleotides into just

00:02:58.368 --> 00:03:01.090
11 synteny blocks.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:07.180
And what is the evolutionary scenario
that nature used to transform

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:12.260
mouse arrangement of blocks into human
arrangement of blocks.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:20.010
You may notice that I show every block
as a directed block, which is oriented

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.700
either to the left or to the right.
And I will explain later

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.620
what precisely these directions mean, but
you may recall that

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.170
two complimentary strands of DNA ran
opposite to each other.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:38.380
And depending on what strand a gene is
located, we may assign

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:43.676
the gene's orientation, left or right, or
plus or minus,

00:03:43.676 --> 00:03:49.270
as we show in this slide.
Now nature doesn't use this

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:54.420
dramatic cut and glue together operations
that I described

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:57.550
when I was explaining the process to my
daughter.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:01.370
It uses a simpler operation called
"reversal".

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:06.358
And reversal simply takes a segment of the
genome and flip it over like

00:04:06.358 --> 00:04:12.210
this, reversing the directions of full
blocks within the segments.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.440
Lets try to see step

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.950
by step of what this particular
evolutionary

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:23.270
scenario for transforming mouse into
human, amounts to.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:27.070
At the first step, we simply revent the
orientation of block 6.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:31.140
In the next step we revert the orientation
of block 9.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.296
Then we take two blocks and

00:04:33.296 --> 00:04:38.980
reverse their orientation, and continue,
continue, continue,

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:43.782
until we transform the mouse gene arrangement
into the human gene

00:04:43.782 --> 00:04:48.780
arrangement on the X chromosome.
I emphasize that this

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.940
is just a hypothetical scenario.
Nobody knows today what

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:59.360
really happened during 75 million years of
evolution while

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:04.340
nature will has been
transforming, mouse gene arrangement

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:09.190
into human gene arrangement.
But if the scenarios that I've

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:14.040
showed here were correct, then one of the
intermediate

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:19.520
arrangements of blocks would correspond to
arrangement of

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.820
blocks on the X chromosome of the
human-mouse ancestor.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.860
That's shown here, and we have to realize
that

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.720
on the way from mouse to the human-mouse
ancestor, we're actually moving in back in

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:40.230
time, and then from the human-mouse
ancestor to human, we are moving

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:46.010
forward 75 million years in time.
Now,

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:51.670
rearrangements are of course dramatic
events happening within genomes.

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:55.490
And you can think about rearrangements as
earthquakes,

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.990
because many bad things may happen.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:02.730
For example, every rearrangement, every
reversal has two end points.

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:08.910
And these endpoints, after a reversal
happens, may actually

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:12.530
disrupt the gene, or they may bring a gene

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:15.920
to a completely foreign territory and put
it under

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:21.281
the influence of the wrong transcription
factor, thus disrupting gene regulations.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:27.510
Now, if rearrangements can be compared to
earthquakes, and we

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:31.090
know that earthquakes are not happening
just at random points,

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.560
for example, where I live near Los Angeles
earthquakes, are

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.400
common, but in most other places they
would be extremely rare.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.370
Well what about genomes?

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:46.820
Are there any rearrangement hotspots or
fragile regions in mammalian genomes

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:51.730
and human genomes in particular?
And also, so far, we have been talking about

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:58.270
evolutionary rearrangements that
happened at the million-year scale.

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:05.500
But rearrangements also happened at much
smaller scale during human development.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:12.040
And we also may ask a question, do
rearrangement hot spots, if they exist,

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:17.200
in mammalian evolutions, correlate with
human rearrangement hotspots

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.200
in fragile regions in humans that we will
be talking about a bit later.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.130
Now, let's talk a little bit whether, in

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:32.230
the scenarios that we described, there are
rearrangement hotspots.

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:34.250
And let's proceed step by step.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:37.266
So this is how our first reversal.
And there

00:07:37.266 --> 00:07:41.094
are two earthquakes corresponding to the
end points

00:07:41.094 --> 00:07:44.360
of this reversal that are shown in this
slide.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.013
Next one, two more earthquakes.

00:07:47.013 --> 00:07:49.249
Next one, two more earthquakes.

00:07:49.249 --> 00:07:50.965
And again every time we perform

00:07:50.965 --> 00:07:54.600
a reversal, there are two earthquakes
happening.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:59.310
Note that I marked the positions of these

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:03.060
earthquakes and points of reversal by vertical

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:07.000
gold streaks, and now we have two gold

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:12.310
streaks in the same regions, this is a
rearrangement hotspot.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:17.710
And by the time we finished transformation
of the mouse X chromosome

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:23.010
into a human X chromosome, we will actually
count three regions

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:28.110
where there will multiply repeated
earthquakes in

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:30.534
the corresponding areas.

00:08:30.534 --> 00:08:36.738
Can we deduce from here that there are
fragile regions in the human genome where

00:08:36.738 --> 00:08:42.600
this rearrangements are happening over and
over again? Of course not.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.619
This is just a hypothetical scenario.

00:08:44.619 --> 00:08:47.068
The real scenario may be completely
different.

00:08:47.068 --> 00:08:51.220
And also, statistically, we cannot make
judgment based on such a small sample.

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:57.440
40 years ago, the prominent biologist Susumu
Ohno, came up

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:01.590
with a random breakage model of
chromosome evolution.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:09.180
Ohno argued that since rearrangements are
so rare, then they must occur at

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:12.210
random positions in the chromosome,
implying that there are

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.660
no fragile regions In human genome.

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:18.670
Honestly, Ohno hardly had any

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:26.110
information to support this model.
But 30 years ago Nadeau &amp; Taylor

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:31.168
generated the first statistical argument
in favor of the Random Breakage Model.

00:09:31.168 --> 00:09:38.780
You may be wondering how one can suggest
statistical argument supporting something

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:40.900
that happened millions of years ago,

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:44.420
without even knowing the evolutionary
scenario

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:51.170
that describe transformation of one genome
into another.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:53.780
Nadeu and Taylor asked a question,

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:58.060
does random breakage model have predictive
power,

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:00.610
despite the fact that according to this model,

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:04.710
rearrangements are happening at
random places.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:08.486
And they suggested the following sort of
experiment.

00:10:08.486 --> 00:10:09.762
Let's apply

00:10:09.762 --> 00:10:14.750
N random reversals to a fake chromosome
consisting

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:19.752
of M genes.
Can we predict how many blocks of a given

00:10:19.752 --> 00:10:25.428
length will be generated as a result of
this random experiment?

00:10:25.428 --> 00:10:31.350
If we can predict it, will this prediction
fit what we observe in real genomes?

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:35.020
And if they do fit what we observe in
real genomes?

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:39.460
Then we have an evidence in support of the
random breakage model.

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:44.900
For example, what would you expect after
applying N random reversals

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:50.130
to a chromosome?
Would we expect roughly the

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:55.280
same number of blocks of every length, or
would we expect

00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:00.230
something like this, where the
number of blocks of each length

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:05.210
will be variable.
It turned out that we expect

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:10.140
something that is similar to this last
slide, and it turned out

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:15.700
that despite the fact that reversals occur
at random positions,

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:21.500
we can predict roughly how many blocks of
each length will be generated.

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:25.830
For example, if we repeat this experiment
100 times,

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:34.010
then the average number of blocks of given
size will follow this distribution.

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:40.280
And this distribution is very well
approximated by the exponential

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:45.298
distribution and an approximation curve as
shown on this slide.

00:11:45.298 --> 00:11:50.990
So when Nadeau and Taylor figured out how
random breakage

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:57.112
model would look like at the simulated
example, they compared

00:11:57.112 --> 00:12:03.590
the synteny block distribution for real human
and mouse data, despite the

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:08.050
fact that in 1984 when their work came
out, there was

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:12.990
very little information about the lengths
of human mouse synteny blocks.

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.476
But even in this time, the lengths

00:12:16.476 --> 00:12:21.480
of known synteny blocks fit exponential
distribution quite well.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:25.253
10 years later,
after the Nadeau and Taylor work,

00:12:25.253 --> 00:12:29.870
when the amount of human-mouse data on
comparative

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:35.110
architecture increase tenfold, people
built the same diagram

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:40.178
and saw that it fits exponential
distribution even better.

00:12:40.178 --> 00:12:41.960
So Nadeau and

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:45.710
Taylor prediction had almost prophetic
power.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:50.950
As a result, starting 1990s, random
breakage model was

00:12:50.950 --> 00:12:57.720
embraced by biologists and has become de
facto theory of chromosome evolution.

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:02.900
And after we described the random breakage
model, we will

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:07.810
look into algorithmic aspects of genome
rearrangement, and

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:10.370
talk about sorting by reversals.

